/* IvaBot DASHBOARD (dashboard-tool.js) v495 - the tool no longer opens in a tab. A tab asked for after two awaits is not a user gesture, so the browser blocked it and the run never happened: the dashboard half filled the page, but nothing was charged, no report was written and the card above View full audit had nothing to announce. The tool now loads in a hidden frame on the dashboard itself, which no blocker touches, so it charges and writes its report whether or not anyone clicks. The card that announces a finished report fired for old reports on every page load, because the first pass marked itself done before any link existed; it now waits until it has seen one. Sentiment in the prompt table is coloured again, accent for positive, pink for negative, grey for neutral, the colours the old cell used before the shared renderer replaced it. v494 - the finished report announces itself above the View full audit link instead of replacing it, so the person can see where the report was put. The card has a tail pointing down at the link, says which tool finished and that the audit can be asked questions in the same chat, and carries the dashboard cross. Clicking it opens the report, the cross closes it, and it leaves on its own after fifteen seconds. Each tool gets its own card over its own link, so running several at once gives several. v493 - the three columns of the run history that were told to shrink to their own text kept wrapping, because the instruction sat on the header row and not on the cells, so a date broke across two lines while the row had space to spare. The three AI cards now say when they were last filled, in the same words and the same grey line the Traffic card uses. The block that used to fill the AI refresh pill is gone, since it carried a price formula from before the rules changed and the pill it wrote to was removed in v491. The other handlers left over from those buttons stay: each is called from five places and each already does nothing when its element is absent, so removing them would mean touching live code for no gain. v492 - a finished report announces itself. The dashboard already noticed new reports every nine hundred milliseconds and simply unhid the View full audit link; now the first time a report appears it puts a small card in the link place, saying which report is ready and offering to open it, and after five seconds the card is removed and the link is back. Reports that already existed when the page loaded are remembered on the first pass and stay quiet. v491 - the page list the run window suggests from was built against a variable that does not exist at that point in the file, so it threw on every render and the field suggested nothing; it reads the user id the way its neighbours do now. The close cross in the window was written as an escape and showed as text. The run button says Run and nothing else. Every remaining way to start a run from inside a page block is gone, the two refresh pills, the two empty-state buttons and the Track a new page button, so the three tool cards are the only entrance. Refreshing newly added keywords keeps its own button, being a separate paid action. v490 - Content Builder is out of the run window, since it writes an article rather than refreshing what the dashboard shows; its card still opens the tool directly. The window no longer opens the tool behind the dashboard back: it goes through the same path the block buttons use, so the page fills on screen first and the report follows. Ticking both tools runs them one after the other, the second starting when the first has finished. v489 - the run window is rebuilt. It is called Track page, because it both adds a page and reruns one already tracked. The address field suggests matching pages as you type, at most five, instead of holding a scrolling list of everything. The three tools are chosen with as many ticks as you like, each showing what it costs for the page in the field, and the total sits in the top right corner and on the button, counting the keywords and prompts that page already carries. Running several tools opens them one after another. v488 - AI Readiness steps by ten prompts, not five: two credits cover a run of up to five prompts, and every ten after that add two more, so fifteen prompts cost four and twenty five cost six. The line above the tool cards says what the section is for, checking a new page or refreshing one already tracked. v487 - the price per run is a formula again, not a flat number, because a flat two credits meant a hundred prompts cost the same as five. Core is one credit plus one for every fifty added keywords. AI Readiness is two credits for a run of up to five prompts and two more for every five after that, so eleven prompts cost six. The three tool cards carry the price in the top right corner, where it can say from, since the exact number depends on the page, and the button keeps the name of the tool and nothing else. v486 - the prompts area carried two more refresh buttons beside the one in the block header, and the line above them still described the old price, three prompts included and a credit for every ten more. Both are gone and the line says what a run costs now, so one button per tool in the block header is the only way to run or refresh. v485 - the window that asks which page to run on now lists the pages already being tracked, as the same selectable row it already uses for choosing the tool; clicking one fills the address field, and a new address can still be pasted instead. The dashboard publishes its page list when it renders so the window can read it. The sentence about new accounts starting with three credits is gone from a screen only reached by people who already have an account and can see their balance in the header. v484 - the line above the tool cards says what the buttons under them now do, pick a tool and the page it runs on, instead of saying a tool can be run on one of your pages from here, which described neither the buttons nor the window they open. v483 - the two run buttons are back on the bottom tool cards, but they now ask which page to run on through the Track a new page window, the same one the empty states already open, because those cards carry no page and a card with a heading, a paragraph and no action is not finished. They show the price like every other run button. The line under the balance says to use credits to refresh your data. v482 - one button per tool instead of a run button and a refresh button. It fills the dashboard on the page first, so the numbers appear while you watch, and then opens the tool, which writes the report. Money moved with it: the dashboard no longer calls charge_credit or refunds, and the tool takes the whole price, so one press is one charge in one place. The refresh window says what it did and what the tool will take. The two run buttons on the bottom tool cards are gone, since running now belongs to a page and those cards carry no page; Content Builder keeps its button. This file must be published together with ai-readiness.js, or runs are free between the two deploys. v481 - one price per tool instead of a formula: AI Readiness takes 2 credits for any run and Core takes 1, however many prompts or keywords the page carries. The places that computed a cost from counts return the flat number now, and every button that writes a price in its own text follows. Checking positions for newly added keywords keeps its own price, because that is a separate action and not a tool run. v480 - the line under the balance says what credits are for and nothing about price, since the price belongs next to the thing being bought. v479 - the credit ladder in the dashboard matches the one agreed on 29 August: 1.667 dollars a credit below 12, 1.5 to 20, 1.364 to 33 and 1.25 from 34, with the saving read as 0, 10, 18 and 25 per cent. The three numbers written into the markup for the opening position of the slider follow it, 8 credits at 12 dollars and 10 per cent rather than 15 and 20. The line under the balance says what a full audit costs instead of one credit is one action. The charge itself is not touched in this file: AI Readiness still takes what its own formula gives it, so this copy and the charge in ai-readiness.js have to go live together. v478 - the version the console prints is the version of the file again; it had been left at v470 while the header moved on, which is the same drift that was fixed once in v444 and makes the console useless for telling which file Webflow is serving. The last system dialog in the dashboard is gone: clicking the brand name opens the field that already sits in that row, prefilled, and Enter renames it, so adding a name and renaming one are the same gesture. The dashboard stylesheet was injected from two places in the same function and the second call could never fire, since the first one leaves the element it checks for; it is removed. v477 - every tracked name looks the same: the brand name now uses the chip the domain and the added names already use, and the pencil is gone, since a row of identical pills should not have one member dressed differently. The name still opens for editing on click and says so on hover. The row ends with how many of the three extra names are in use. A name is normalised when it is saved, every run of spaces, tabs and non-breaking spaces collapses to one space, the same way a prompt already is, so a stray double space cannot stop the exact-text comparison from matching. v476 - a brand can be tracked under several names. Under the brand chip sits a row of chips: the domain, which is always counted and carries no cross, and up to three names you add, each with the cross the rest of the dashboard uses; adding is the same link and input pattern as Add prompt. The names are kept per user and per domain in the brand map that already syncs to dash_state, so no new key and no migration. One list is read once per run and feeds both places that decide whether an answer named the brand, the dot on the row and the row written to prompt_answers, so the table and the card can no longer disagree. The count on the Brand mentions card still asks DataForSEO for one name and is untouched until the comparison between the domain and the word has been run. v475 - windows have three sizes and nothing else: 420 for a confirmation, 660 for a short list such as competitors or sources, 1080 for a table with dates, tabs or engine answers. All three are written as min(width, 92vw) in the stylesheet, so a phone gets the width of its screen without any code deciding it. Two places used to write a pixel width straight onto the element, which beat every class and is why the history window was 1080 whatever the markup said; they pick a step now. The competitors window went back to 660 because the table inside it carries a 520 minimum and 560 left less room than that, which is where the sideways scroll came from. v474 - the competitors window is 560 wide instead of 1080, since it holds a domain and a cross, and each domain sits in the same iva-clip cell the backlink tables use, so a long address is clipped inside its column rather than stretching the row. Run history: Date, Audit and Tool shrink to their own text and Page takes the remaining width, so the last column now ends at the edge of the table instead of trailing into empty space, the pager sits directly under the last row, and the column called Action is called Tool, which is what it holds. A window opened through openReal can now ask for its own width and everything else keeps the old 1080. v473 - the competitors window uses the cross the dashboard already uses to remove a row, right aligned in its own narrow column with the accent on hover, instead of the words Remove from competitors repeated down the page. v472 - ten prompts to a page instead of five. The competitor and source lists open in the dashboard card rather than a system alert: the same openReal window the old table already used, domains as link rows. A competitor carries Remove from competitors, which drops that domain for the whole site and for every prompt on it, so a partner or a shop never comes back as a rival. The exclusions are stored per user and per domain in dash_state next to brand, prompts, added and locale, so they follow the account across browsers, and no table or column was added. The number in the Comp. column counts what is left after the removals. v471 - paging is one component everywhere. The prompt table built its rows from the full list and sliced the copy afterwards, so every prompt stayed on screen and the page buttons changed nothing; it slices first now. The keyword table had a second pager of its own, drawn inside the table with smaller buttons and no Showing line, and it now uses the same pagerHTML, pagerGo and pgbtn as the prompt table, the run history and the site list. Named without a link moved out of the pager container into its own block. Charts read the floorZero flag that was already being passed to them, so a trend that cannot go below nought starts at nought instead of at its own lowest value. The brand mentions chart opens on 30 days, matching the card above it. v470 - the page buttons are pinned to thirty-two pixels in the stylesheet with padding and box sizing fixed, so a button cannot grow from the text or the padding of whatever wraps it; that is why the prompt pager looked larger than the keyword one although both use the same class. The line repeating the run date under the table is gone, the header already says when the run was. v469 - paging works and there is only one pager. The page buttons called the render function of the first mount, so a later card changed its page number and redrew someone else's rows; the function is now rebound on every mount. The old table kept its own pager visible next to the new one, which is why two different button sizes appeared; it is hidden with the table it belongs to. The chart scale reads 0 of 4 engines per check, and before there is a line it says the trend will build up over the next few checks. v468 - Gemini is named everywhere the engines are listed, in the running text, the tooltips, the history columns and the CSV header, and the label that called Gemini Google AI is corrected so the two are no longer the same word. The per-prompt chart says what it shows: Mentions or Citations across all checks, with nought to four engines per check underneath, and before a second check it promises the trend rather than asking for one. v467 - AI Readiness and Core no longer touch: the block that moves to the top now carries padding, a margin and a hairline underneath, so the two tools are separated the way the rest of the page separates sections. Paging sits directly under the table with the run date below it, matching the tracked-keywords block, instead of below a line of text. v466 - paging is the dashboard's own pagerHTML with its Showing x of y line, arrows, ellipsis and pgbtn buttons, not the row of plain numbers I had built. Chart points drop to radius two so the line reads as a small companion to the row. Under the table sits Named without a link: the prompts where an engine said the brand and linked somewhere else in that run, with the engines that did it and who they linked to instead, which is the shortest path from a mention to a citation. v465 - the per-prompt chart now copies the Brand mentions trend rather than the keyword one: accent line, ten per cent accent fill and points of radius three, not the lilac line with radius five. Same colours and point size as the chart above it, only shorter, so nothing in the row competes with it. v464 - the per-prompt trend is the chart the dashboard already draws, not a hand-made one. It copies the keyword detail chart exactly: the same white card, the same Chart.js line with the lilac fill, the same point size, tick colours and grid, only shorter so it does not dominate the row. The scale runs nought to four, a click on a point still opens that run. The hand-rolled SVG is gone. v463 - the per-prompt trend sits in a white card with a border, like every other chart in the dashboard, and it now says what it measures: a scale of nought to four down the left with grid lines, the first and last check dates under the ends of the line, and a line explaining that each point is one check and a click opens that run. The line spans the full width of the card instead of floating in the middle of empty space. v462 - an open prompt carries its own trend under the answer: how many of the four engines named or cited it on each check, following whichever side of the toggle is active. A point carries its date and score on hover and loads that run when clicked, and the selected run is the filled dark point. With a single check there is no line, only a note that a second check is needed, so a straight segment is never mistaken for a trend. v461 - the prompt table came back. It referenced COPY, the icon variable that belongs to a different inline script, so every row threw COPY is not defined and the table fell back to the old empty one; the icon is written into the row markup instead, and the click still goes to the shared ivaCopyCell handler, which does live on window. The only outside name the table now uses is that handler. v460 - the shared table replaces the old one only when the card actually has tracked prompts, so a card with none keeps what it had instead of showing an empty frame, and the console names how many prompts the mount found. The AI Readiness heading on the page-level card is the same twenty pixels as everywhere else; it sits in its own markup and had been missed twice. v459 - the prompt now uses the copy control the dashboard already had, the same icon and the same ivaCopyCell handler, instead of a new one. Markdown left in the answers is cleaned as well as the footnote markers: hash headings and asterisk bullets become plain text and proper bullets. Both section headings sit at twenty pixels, the size of the Sites you are working on title, rather than a size of their own. Only the tooltips that existed before are shown; the ones invented for Page and Google AI are gone. v458 - tooltip marks sit to the right of the header word on one line, as they do in every other block, instead of wrapping underneath. A prompt and its answer are tinted as one group across the whole row, with no accent bar. Copy is an icon after the prompt rather than the word copy in the text flow; it writes to the clipboard and shows a tick, falling back to a window where the clipboard is blocked. Both section headings are twice their old size so AI Readiness and Core read as separate tools. v457 - the merged table gets what the old one had and what it was missing. Tooltips are back on Page, Google AI, Sentiment, Comp. and Src. The prompt column is the widest at thirty percent, shows more of the question and carries a copy link, with the whole prompt on hover. The calendar closes on a second click. Footnote markers such as the bracketed numbers Perplexity leaves behind are stripped from the answer. A prompt and its answer read as one group, tinted and marked with an accent bar down the left. Section headings are larger so AI Readiness and Core read as different tools, and the card is named Cited from your prompts to match the table. v456 - one prompt table instead of two. The renderer written for the All prompt runs modal now draws the table on the dashboard as well, with the same calendar, search, toggle, answers, delete and tooltips, so there is no second version to keep in step; the old table is hidden rather than deleted, and the Show all prompt runs link is hidden with it. Five prompts to a page, paged with the same pgbtn buttons the other blocks use. v455 - AI Readiness now sits above Core in the detail view, moved in the page when it renders rather than by rewriting the markup, so the change is in one place and easy to undo. The two mention numbers are told apart by name: the card reads Brand mentions on the web, counted across the web by DataForSEO, while the table is called Prompt tracking and counts only the prompts you track. Gemini is named in the tooltips alongside the other three. v454 - the history only records real runs. A sandbox run writes no snapshot at all, so its invented numbers can never reach a chart or a card, and a run where no engine returned an answer writes nothing either instead of saving a row of zeros. The brand snapshot follows the same rule and is skipped when the count is zero or the run is a sandbox one. Six test and empty snapshots recorded on 27 Aug were removed from the Suave history by hand. v453 - the answer formatter was deleted along with the block it lived in when that block was rewritten, so every answer row threw fmtAns is not defined; that also swallowed the click that expands a prompt, which is why no other row would open. The function is back above its first use, and the renderer is now executed against sample data before release rather than only parsed. v452 - the answer no longer depends on the database replying. Every prompt already carries an excerpt of its latest answer in the dashboard state, so that is shown at once and marked as an excerpt; the full text with engine tabs replaces it if the query returns. A run that came back with no rows keeps the excerpt instead of claiming there is no answer. One console line now names the modal, the run date and the prompt count as soon as it opens. v451 - the first prompt shows its answer and the rest open on an arrow, so the table does not run on forever. The answer loader now says what happened: a failed query, a rejected request or a throw all end with an empty result and a console line, and a run that is still loading after eight seconds stops waiting instead of leaving Loading on every row. v450 - answers are shown under every prompt straight away instead of waiting for a click, loaded in one query for the whole run rather than one per prompt. The text is formatted rather than dumped: the leaked message text prefix is stripped, bold titles are rendered, numbered points and dashes start on their own line, and anything past four hundred characters folds behind Show more per prompt. Each answer has its own engine tabs. Clicking a long prompt still opens it in full to copy. v449 - the Citations side of the toggle had nothing to show because only mentions were stored per run; every run now records citations per engine as well, so the toggle changes the dots instead of only the buttons. Dates checked before this version have mentions only and stay empty under Citations. The dashboard prompt table gains its own search box above it, and the answer loader reports in the console how many answers came back for a prompt. v448 - the page column in the prompt runs modal is a link icon with the address on hover, which frees the width the full URL was eating. The date button opens a month calendar instead of stepping through runs one click at a time: days with a run carry a dot, the selected day is filled, and clicking a day loads that run. Removing a prompt in this table removes it from tracking everywhere, since both tables read the same list. v447 - the All prompt runs modal is drawn by its own renderer. It has a search box and a run picker on the right of the toggle, one row per prompt, the answer opening under the row it belongs to with a tab per engine and a Show more link, competitor and source lists behind their chips, a long prompt opening in full so it can be copied, and a cross that removes a prompt from tracking. The generic history renderer stays for every other report. v446 - the All prompt runs table now carries one row per prompt, not one row per prompt per date, so a prompt never repeats down the page. It shows the latest run, named in the title, with the four engines, sentiment, competitors and sources; Page is a link to the page that was cited. Search, the date picker, the answer under each row and the per-prompt graph follow in the next steps. Page code for this block is written plainly and escaped programmatically, which is what the last two broken releases were caused by. v445 - v444 shipped broken: two closing brackets were missing in the Gemini and Google AI cells of the All prompt runs table, which threw at parse time and stopped the whole dashboard script, so the site list disappeared. The file itself passed a syntax check because the fault sat inside the page document, not in the outer file; every inline script is now checked separately before a file is sent. v444 - the console line that reports the rendered version said v429 no matter which file was live, because the number was written into the string once and never touched again; it now matches the file, so the console can be trusted to tell which version Webflow is serving. Three fixes on the prompt tables. Column widths are rebalanced so the tooltip marks no longer sit on top of the header next to them. The group strip that reads Added by you and Selected by IvaBot spans all eleven columns again instead of stopping short. The All prompt runs modal carries the same columns as the dashboard: Gemini, Google AI, sentiment, competitors and sources. The date format of that modal is unchanged for now; one row per prompt with dates as tabs comes next. v443 - the prompt table gains a Sources column next to Competitors, with the same chip and the count in it, and the sandbox message no longer contradicts itself by saying a credit was used. Competitors and sources are split for the table exactly as they are split for the database: commercial domains on one side, reference sites such as reddit, app stores, forums, news, government and .org pages on the other, and search or assistant redirect hosts dropped from both. v442 - a sandbox mode for testing without spending. Open the dashboard with ?iva_sandbox=1 and every DataForSEO call goes to their sandbox, which answers with the same structure for free, no credit is charged, and the closing message says the answers are test data. It stays on until ?iva_sandbox=0. A real user never passes the parameter, so their runs are live and billed exactly as before. The flag travels in each request body rather than in a shared variable, so a test can never route someone else to fake data. v441 - an empty run no longer erases a good one. When every engine comes back with no answer text, which happens when two refreshes overlap and DataForSEO refuses the simultaneous live tasks, the result of that day is left as it was instead of being overwritten with no-one-found. When answers do arrive, an engine that was already true for that day stays true. v440 - the prompt table gains a fourth engine column. The column that read Google AI was in fact showing Gemini, so it is renamed Gemini, and Google AI now means the AI Overview in Google search, which is a different surface and gets its own dot. The overview result of each run is written onto that run, so the column carries real data rather than a default. Column widths redistributed across ten columns. v439 - adding a prompt that asks the same thing as one already tracked now asks first. Words are compared with the filler removed, so suave pay later is recognised as the same question as what is Suave pay later, which is how Suave ended up paying for nine prompts that are seven questions. An auto-added prompt is stored under the same lower-case key as everything else; it used the raw text before, so the same prompt in different case created a second entry. v438 - a run that returns nothing costs nothing. The credit is still charged at confirmation, and if the check comes back with no data at all, or if it was skipped because another run for the same domain was still going, the credit is returned through add_credits with a refund marker in the identifier, the balance on screen is corrected and the closing message says the credit came back rather than that it was used. v435 verified on a live run and v436 diagnostics answered: the brand from the card does reach the check, and the variants now include suave, so mentions are counted correctly. v437 - a second Refresh started while the first is still running now stops at the door. Overlapping runs came back with empty answers on every prompt, most likely against the DataForSEO limit on simultaneous live tasks, while the credit had already been charged at confirmation, so the run cost money and returned nothing. A lock per domain holds for the length of the run and is released at every exit, so sequential runs are unaffected. v436 - one diagnostic line in the console per run, printing the brand name as the code receives it, the host, and the full list of brand variants used to decide whether an answer mentions the brand. The card shows Suave Pay while the mention count behaves as though the brand were the domain, so the two need to be compared on a live account before the brand handling is finalised. v435 - the brand is now also recognised without the wrapper words of its own domain. paywithsuave.com yields suave, gettrykla-style prefixes are stripped the same way, and that token joins the list of brand variants. Answers say Suave far more often than they say Pay with Suave, so the previous run still recorded a prompt as not mentioned while the answer named the brand in its first sentence. v434 - brand mentions were undercounted because the brand was matched literally. An answer that says Pay with Suave did not match paywithsuave, so a prompt could be cited and still recorded as not mentioned. Both the run dot and the mentioned column now also compare with spaces and punctuation removed on both sides, so the wording of the answer no longer decides whether a mention is seen. v433 - citations are read from the annotations array instead of the answer text. DataForSEO returns the sources of an answer in items[].sections[].annotations[], each with a url and a title that holds the domain name, and that array is where Perplexity and Gemini keep their links, which is why cited stayed false on every one of their rows while ChatGPT, which writes URLs into the answer body, worked. cited, cited_urls and the page of the run now come from the text and the annotations together, and a domain named in an annotation title also counts towards the competitor list, which is the only way Gemini contributes one at all. v432 - the competitor list is cleaned against the first real run. Financial media and advice sites such as cnbc, forbes, investopedia, nerdwallet and creditkarma move to sources, since they answer the question rather than compete for the customer. Search and assistant redirect hosts, vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com above all, are dropped from both lists: Gemini returns its citations wrapped in that host, so it was the only competitor on every Gemini row. v431 - sentiment, competitors and sources now reach prompt_answers. Every row carries the domains found in that engine's answer, split into competitors, meaning commercial domains, and sources, meaning reference domains such as reddit, app stores, forums and .gov or .edu addresses. Sentiment is written back onto the rows of the same run as soon as the batch sentiment step returns, since the rows are inserted before that step runs and used to keep sentiment null forever. Requires the columns competitors and sources on prompt_answers, added 27 Aug. v430 - AI Readiness data fixes. Gemini model name is now gemini-2.5-flash: DataForSEO rejected gemini-1.5-flash with 40501 Invalid Field 'model_name', which is why no gemini row was ever written. The AI Overview citation sub-check now calls the perplexity engine with a model name instead of engine 'sonar', which returned 40402 Invalid Path, and passes model_name for chat_gpt, which returned 40501 without it. The AI Overview check now runs on the tracked prompts (up to 10) and falls back to ranked keywords only when no prompt is tracked, so the Google AI column measures the same questions as the other engines. v429 - the header tooltips wrap again: the header row carries white-space:nowrap and the ::after box inherited it, so the text ran out of the white box in one line. v428 - AI Readiness prompt table: the Competitors header no longer runs over the Answer column, and the tooltips on the last two headers open to the left so they stay inside the card. The table gained a ninth column in v427 while the inline widths and the injected stylesheet still described eight, so Competitors was left with 7 percent for a label that needs twice that, the Answer column inherited the 6 percent meant for the remove cross, and the remove column had no width at all. v427 - built on v416, additive only. Keyword table: movement arrow beside the newest position, comparing it with the previous kept position, and the table scrolls inside its box below 1100px instead of spilling out of the card. Backlinks: New domains and Lost domains tabs between the newest saved list and the one before it. AI prompts: only the model's own message is saved to the prompt_answers table, without the source titles the API appends, and a Read answer column opens it with one tab per engine, showing the date, model, web-search flag, whether the page was cited or only the brand named, the brand sentence highlighted and the cited URLs. Console: the audit-links line prints only when its numbers change. v416 - the keyword table keeps a dated column only when a position actually changed against the previous kept column, so a row of identical runs collapses into one column carrying a x N marker and a tooltip naming the merged dates. Several runs on one day used to land in the same column and a month of unchanged Labs data used to read as six identical columns, which made it impossible to tell a working refresh from a broken one. Show all data still lists every run. v415 - the SEO score gauge fills completely at 100. The arc path spans 172 units between its endpoints, so its real radius is 86 and its length 270.2, while the stroke dash array was calculated from a radius of 78 and a length of 245.0; a perfect score therefore drew only 90.7 percent of the ring. v414 - the in-dashboard Core and AI refreshes no longer send a fixed US/en locale. The country and language are read from the newest snapshot that carries them, written by the full Core Audit run, and cached per domain, so a refresh stops overwriting a Romanian or Dutch account with American data. v413 - the in-dashboard Core refresh no longer writes p_keywords_checked as null. That single null wiped the PAGE IS BUILT FOR block on every refresh, so those rows never moved no matter how many times Refresh was pressed; they only changed when the full Core Audit tool was run. The list is now carried over from the last core snapshot and its positions are re-checked live through the keyword_positions mode. v412 - DM Sans is loaded from Google Fonts. The previous @font-face rules pointed src at a bare Webflow asset id instead of a file URL, returned 404, and left the whole dashboard in the browser default sans-serif. v402 - statusChip, sentCell and compCell moved out of fillPromptsDomain into the enclosing scope, so fillAir can call them on inner pages. Before this the render loop threw ReferenceError: statusChip is not defined on the first non-home page and stopped, leaving later pages undrawn. v401 - the AI Readiness prompt table stylesheet is applied before the render loop instead of after it, so the fixed column widths and the centred cells always take effect and each value sits under its own heading. The Page heading is centred to match its column. v400 - AI Readiness prompt table: the Page column renders the chain-link icon again instead of printing the raw SVG markup as text, so the column no longer stretches past the card. The header logo now carries a real href to the home page, so it works even when the JS handler has not been wired yet. v399 - after a refresh only the pages that were open before stay open, instead of also force-opening the freshest page and so showing extra panels; the freshest page opens only when nothing was remembered. A remembered open page also floats to the top of its site so it is not pushed down the list by pages with a newer snapshot and lost below the fold. v398 - two fixes. The IvaBot logo returns to the home page again: window.top.location is blocked silently in a cross-origin frame, so it falls back to window.open(_top) then to a same-frame navigation. The status dots sit in a full-width flex box, so filled and hollow markers centre under their header and the column reads as a straight vertical line. v397 - the IvaBot logo again returns to the home page: window.top.location is blocked silently when the dashboard runs in a cross-origin frame, so it now falls back to window.open(_top) and then to a same-frame navigation. The page-cited link is a drawn SVG chain icon in the muted grey, replacing the unicode link character that rendered as an empty lavender square where the font lacked the glyph. v396 - the page column uses a chain-link icon, which reads as open this page rather than the diagonal arrow that reads as leave for another site. The three engine columns are widened to match the width of their headers, ChatGPT 8, Perplexity 9, Google AI 9 percent, so the status dot sits under the centre of its label instead of looking shifted; the prompt column gives back the difference at 38 percent. The dots were already centre-aligned like the numeric columns in the keyword table; only the column widths were off. v395 - the column shares now come from the width each header needs, so a label no longer spills out of its own cell and drifts away from the data underneath: prompt 41 percent, page 7, ChatGPT 7, Perplexity 8, Google AI 8, sentiment 10, competitors 13, remove 6. Headers stay on one line, Google AI included, and their tooltips open to the right of the marker only. The page column is a link icon rather than a path, with the address in the tooltip and in the link. v394 - the prompt table goes back to a fixed layout, which is the only arrangement where the columns cannot add up to more than the width available, so nothing hangs outside the card and nothing has to be scrolled sideways, on a phone as much as on a desktop. The shares are set for reading rather than for symmetry: the prompt takes 37.5 percent, the page 15, each engine 5.5, sentiment 10, competitors 15 and the remove column 6. Page cited now shows the path alone, since the domain is already on the card above, with the whole address in the tooltip and in the link. v393 - the ceilings in the prompt table now sit on the cells themselves, with the ellipsis and the no-wrap on the cell, which is how the keyword table has always done it. They were on the inner block before, so the cell still asked for the full width of its text, the table grew wider than its box and the remove column hung outside the border. Prompt 460px, page 240px, competitors 200px. v392 - column headers in the prompt table carry their alignment in the markup instead of relying on the injected stylesheet, so each header sits over its own data rather than drifting to the middle of the column, and they align to the bottom of the header row. The remove column in both the prompt table and the keyword table is 44px wide with 14px to the edge, so the crosses stand on the same line in Core and in AI Readiness and no longer touch the border. v391 - the question-mark tooltips in the prompt table open to the side of the marker and are readable in full. They were cut off because the clipping added in v386 and v389 covered the header row and the box around the table; clipping now applies to body cells only, where the ellipsis is needed, and the tooltips of the last three columns open to the left so they stay inside the card. v390 - the prompt table now sizes itself the way the keyword table does: no percentage widths and no fixed layout, columns take the room their content needs, and the long cells carry their own ceiling instead, 460px for the prompt cell and its text, 220px for the page and 200px for the competitors, each cut with an ellipsis and holding the full text in its tooltip. The engine and sentiment columns stay on one line. This replaces the fixed grid that squeezed a prompt into a word and a half while the row had space to spare. v389 - four things. Add keywords is now bound to its own page on every page row: the markup handler looked the box up across the whole document, so on any account with more than one tracked page it opened the first page's field and appeared dead. The prompt table carries table-layout fixed and max-width in its own markup and its box clips, so a cell can no longer push the table past the card even if the injected stylesheet is missing. Page cited drops the query string, cuts at 28 characters and is a link that opens in a new tab, with the full address in the tooltip. Prompts may now run to 25 words and 200 characters. v388 - the prompt table no longer carries a minimum width and its box no longer scrolls sideways: columns shrink with the window and every cell clips to its column, with the full prompt in the hover tooltip that was already there. Adding a prompt now accepts two to fifteen words and at most 120 characters, so a pasted paragraph cannot land in the table in the first place. v387 - the prompt block says when it was last checked, on one line above the table, read from the date of the last AI Readiness snapshot, so it reports when the data was actually fetched rather than when a prompt row was seeded; it reads Not checked yet before the first run. The table keeps its eight columns, the date is not a column. v386 - the prompt table clips overflow on every cell from the stylesheet, instead of depending on each cell builder to wrap its content in a hidden-overflow div, which is why long prompts and competitor lists kept escaping the card whenever a column was added or rebuilt. The table also carries a minimum width in CSS and its box scrolls sideways below that. v385 - the prompt table in AI Readiness keeps a minimum width and its box scrolls sideways, so the Competitors and remove columns stay inside the card instead of running past its edge on a narrow window. v384 - two things. Custom range is back on the Core trend with a real calendar, ported from the working one in AI Readiness: it dots the days that actually have snapshots, takes a start and an end, and filters the series; the presets clear it again. And a regression from v380 is fixed: PAGE IS BUILT FOR was collecting the checked keywords of every run made on the latest day, which turned three keywords into twelve on paywithsuave.com and pushed a pager under that group. It now reads the single most recent run that carries them. v383 - the Traffic / Positions / Backlinks button row under the trend is gone. Traffic is a fourth card next to Keyword positions, Backlinks and SEO score, first in the row, showing estimated organic traffic from the latest snapshot with its change against the previous one. Clicking any card switches the trend as before, and the selected card is highlighted from one place. v382 - the line under every trend title now states the period on screen and follows the range picker: All time reads as every saved check, a preset reads as the last 30 days or the last 3 months, a custom pair reads as the two dates. Applies to the Core trend on all three metrics and to the Brand mentions chart in AI Readiness. v381 - Backlinks card now shows the change against the previous Core snapshot, in the same style as the Keyword positions card: more links reads as up, fewer as down, nothing when unchanged. fillBl takes the backlink history as a seventh argument for this. v380 - fixes the stray closing brace introduced in v379 that ended fillKw early and threw Unexpected token 'function', which stopped the dashboard script and left the sites list empty. No data was affected. v379 - Core trend now opens on Estimated organic traffic instead of Average Google position, so a rising line always means growth: average position improves when weak keywords drop out, which read as growth while the page was losing visibility. A metric switcher under the trend header (Traffic / Positions / Backlinks, styled as the existing filter buttons) keeps the other views one click away, and the cards still switch the chart as before. The range picker on the Core trend is wired to real snapshots: 30 days, 3 months, 6 months and All time now filter the series, defaulting to All time so nothing disappears from view; Custom range is hidden until its calendar is wired. Keyword positions card reads ranked_keywords_count from the latest snapshot and shows the change against the previous one, instead of counting the union of keywords across every visible snapshot, which is why it showed 144 while the last run found 107. The snapshot query now also selects est_organic_traffic and ranked_keywords_count. Brand mentions chart in AI Readiness no longer merges several runs from the same day into one point, matching the Core charts. v378 - trend charts now draw every saved snapshot in order instead of the last six with same-day runs merged into one point. Core positions, backlinks, SEO score and AI mentions each plot one point per run, so several runs on the same day stay visible and every date is on the axis. Snapshots older than the last six no longer fall out of the chart: on paywithsuave.com the 14, 18 and 19 July points had disappeared because five runs on 20 July filled the window. Only the four history-window expressions changed; data sources, tables and cards are untouched. v371 - tracked brand, prompts and added keywords now live in the new dash_state table in Supabase, not only in the browser, so they follow the account across browsers and devices. localStorage stays as the fast local copy: on load the database wins, and if the database has nothing while the browser does, the browser copy is pushed up once so nothing is lost. Saves go to both. v370 - the Core fix list now has the same three columns as the AI Readiness one, What to fix / What to do / Priority, reading the what-to-do line saved by core-tool v255. The AI table's first column was renamed from Signal to What to fix so both read identically. Runtime version label corrected, it still said v365. v369 - SEO score panel has two states after a run, the list of fixes or Great work, and asks for a Core Audit run when there is nothing saved yet. The earlier wording about a checklist saved by a later version was internal detail the reader does not need. v368 - SEO score panel: a page whose last Core snapshot predates seo-tools v112 has no saved checklist at all, and the panel was reading that missing list as an empty one and congratulating the user. It now separates the two cases and asks for one more Core run. v367 - SEO score panel now lists what to fix on the page, read from coverage.bad on the Core snapshot (needs seo-tools v112, which started saving it), sorted by priority and styled with the same Critical / Important / Nice to have colours as the AI Readiness report. The score-over-time chart is no longer shown on that tab, since the list is what people act on. Range wording unified across the dashboard to 7 days / 14 days / 30 days / 3 months / 6 months / 12 months / All time. v366 - On-page AI score now lists what to fix instead of sending the user to Core Audit: the panel reads coverage.bad from the last AI Readiness snapshot and names the signals still missing (schema, FAQ markup and so on), on the homepage and on internal pages, through the shared airFixList helper. Core Audit was the wrong destination, since these signals are scored by AI Readiness. On-page AI score table also carries a Priority column (Critical / Important / Nice to have, taken from the AI Readiness severities) and sorts by it. Brand mentions panel gained the month-by-month trend chart it had only been promising in text, plus a range picker (3, 6, 12 months, all time, or a custom from-to pair): it draws from the ai_mentions_count points already collected in pg.aiHist, rendered on first open of the Brand tab so Chart.js gets a sized container. Nothing else changed. v365 - Page cited: the placeholder dash no longer carries its own inline centering, so the whole column (header, real URLs, placeholder) follows the single .iva-ptbl left-align rule and stops drifting. Nothing else changed. v364 - backlinks: the expanded table now has two tabs, Backlinks (itemized links, as before) and Referring domains (grouped by domain with the link count per domain, read from the snapshot rows marked kind:"domain"); Download CSV writes one file with both blocks. Fixes the dashboard-level Download CSV for backlinks and for keywords, where the selector looked for backlinks.csv / positions.csv while the markup calls ivaDlCSV, so the real-data handler was never bound and the click fell through to the demo HIST object. Page cited: the placeholder dash is centred in its cell, real URLs stay left-aligned. ivaDoCore now also pulls referring_domains so an in-dashboard Core refresh writes both lists. ivaDoCore now computes the snapshot avg_position over ALL keywords (ranked from DFS + tracked/added, deduped per keyword) instead of ranked-only, so the positions trend counts every keyword going forward. top3/top10 stay ranked-only. The trend chart is left on the frozen per-snapshot avg (corePts=h.avg unchanged), so past points stay put and deleting a keyword only affects the next snapshot, not history. Also removed the false hardcoded 'refreshed 2 days ago, within the 5-day window' line in the refresh modal. Home cards unchanged (one data-ai="brand", one data-ai="cited"). v362 - empty Core state (ivaCoreSkeleton): Run Core Audit button is now wrapped in the SAME white framed card as the AI Run-AI-Readiness button (background #fff, 1px separator border, radius 12, padding 22, margin-top 4, centered, with a No-Core-data-yet line above it) and appended at the END of the Core block. v361 had moved it to the end but left it as a bare centered button with no frame; now the frame and placement match AI Readiness exactly. Home cards unchanged (one data-ai=brand, one data-ai=cited). v361 - empty Core state (ivaCoreSkeleton): the 'Run Core Audit \u00b7 1 credit' button now sits at the END of the Core block (appended after the keyword/backlinks detail), matching where 'Run AI Readiness' sits at the end of the AI block, instead of high up right under the Core cards. Only the insertion point and top margin changed; no data-source, template, or home-card changes (still one data-ai="brand", one data-ai="cited"). v360 - empty-state run buttons now show the price consistently: 'Run Core Audit \u00b7 1 credit' and 'Run AI Readiness \u00b7 1 credit' (first run = 1 credit, includes 3 prompts for AI). v359 - empty Core state: the 'Run Core Audit' button is moved out of the Keyword-positions tab panel to a centered spot right under the Core cards, so it is visible on every tab (Keyword positions / Backlinks / SEO score), matching how the AI 'Run AI Readiness' button is always visible. The kw-table empty row is now text-only; the single .iva-runcore button opens the Core tool with autorun. v358 - fix: empty Core state (ivaCoreSkeleton) now also clears the backlinks block, which was showing static demo rows (healthline-style-blog.com etc.) as 'real data' on pages where Core was never run. It blanks the bl table to a 'No Core data yet' row and hides the real-data chip, Show all and Download CSV for that state. Also supersedes the mistaken v357 top Run-Core button (built from clean v356: top #coreRefreshBtn stays hidden pre-run, the native bottom .iva-runcore button handles first run, Refresh returns after a run). v356 - auto-row (Selected by IvaBot) Page cited placeholder dash is now centered (text-align:center, padding 9px 10px) so it lines up like the other placeholder dashes instead of hugging the left. v355 - internal-page AI Readiness cards are now real tabs: 'Cited by AI' and 'On-page AI score' get class iva-aic2 + data-ai2, wired through the existing _st2 switcher to two data-tc2 panels (cited = the citation note + homepage-prompts-that-cite-this-page table; onpage = on-page signals detail with an advisory line that points to Core Audit when signals are missing or says good job when all are in place). Default tab 'cited', remembered per page via _aiTab. Mirrors the home On-page AI score panel from v352. No data-source or edge changes. v354 - AI Readiness prompt table polish: Sentiment now shows a value only when the brand actually appeared in that prompt's latest run (was showing e.g. Positive on prompts where you were never cited); otherwise it shows a dash. Home prompt table (.iva-ptbl) body cells for Prompt / Page cited / Competitors switched from padding:9px 6px to 9px 10px so they align under the 10px header (were shifted 4px left). Sentiment and Competitors headers gained ? tooltips explaining each, on both the homepage table and the internal page table. Applies to home and internal via the shared sentCell. v353 - AI Readiness in-dashboard refresh (ivaDoAir) parallelised: per prompt the 3 engines (ChatGPT/Perplexity/Google AI) now run via Promise.all, and prompts run through a concurrency pool of 3 (max ~9 concurrent dataforseo-proxy calls) instead of ~27 sequential awaits, so a 9-prompt refresh drops from dozens of seconds to a few batches. Accumulators (citeCount, per-rec runs/competitors/_stext, cited page) and the batched air-gpt step=sentiment call after the loop are unchanged; result combine is engine-order-deterministic. No edge-function changes. v352 - home AI card now shows a third tab, On-page AI score (data-ai="onpage"), alongside Brand mentions and Cited by AI; it mirrors the internal On-page AI score card exactly (reads pg.airPassed / pg.airTotal from the AI Readiness snapshot coverage) and carries its own data-tc="onpage" panel with an advisory line that points to Core Audit when signals are missing or says good job when all are in place. Home integrity unchanged (one data-ai="brand", one data-ai="cited"). v351 - prompt table rebuilt to 8 cols (adds Sentiment + Competitors), curly quotes removed, group labels now caps+grey via .iva-ptbl tr.grp td (rule was .cmp-scoped only), page-cited markdown-link cleanup, page delete now actually fires + clears snapshots/runs so pages stop reappearing (builder_articles untouched), +N competitors opens a modal, pager active colour unified #151415, loading copy notes up to a minute. v350 - sentiment: AI refresh captures a response snippet per prompt and makes ONE batched air-gpt step=sentiment call to classify each as positive/neutral/negative, stored as rec.sentiment; internal table shows a Sentiment column (positive=accent, neutral=muted, negative=pinkdn) between Google AI and Competitors. Requires air-gpt v5 (sentiment step deployed). v349 - prompt status chips (new / improved / dropped, stable = none) computed from rec.runs (last two runs), shown inline next to the prompt on the internal table like the keyword chip; dropped uses pinkdn. v348 - homepage prompt table now shows a competitors cell (compCell: first cited-instead domain + '+N') per prompt, matching the internal table; group/empty colspans widened 6->7. v347 - internal AI cards reordered to match Core layout: 'Cited by AI' first, 'On-page AI score' second (score last, like Core's SEO score). v346 - delete now means delete: removing a page or site also deletes its snapshots from the DB (scoped strictly to the user's user_id), so it no longer reappears when re-run; confirm copy updated to 'delete ... cannot be undone'. v345 - internal page now shows an 'On-page AI score' card (signals_passed / signals_total from the AI Readiness snapshot coverage) next to 'Cited by AI', matching the agreed mockup. v344 - clutter cleanup step 1: fillKw now clears the demo keyword placeholder (collagen rows + fake '42' count) and shows an empty state when the page has no real ranked keywords, instead of leaving the demo visible. Backlinks/AI/trend demo cleanup next. v343 - AI section copy corrected from 'first 5 prompts included' to 'first 3' (matches pricing). NOTE known issue for next pass: when a page has no real keyword data (empty snapshot), the static collagen demo placeholder stays visible because fillKw returns early without clearing it — needs demo-seed removal + empty states (clutter cleanup #4). v342 - CRITICAL FIX: keyword and backlink tables threw 'rec is not defined' because a global 'auto' badge edit (v328) wrongly injected rec._auto into their rowHtml (row vars are r and b, not rec); reverted those two to the plain new-badge, prompt rowHtml unchanged. This restores Core keyword/backlink rendering and the delete (Remove) buttons. v341 - internal page final model (Cited-by-AI 'this page' + prompt table only when cited, no per-page refresh <3 prompts), AI refresh parses competitor domains per prompt (rec.competitors), internal table shows 'Competitors cited' column (clickable domain + '+N'). NOTE: versions v337-v341 code landed but the version label had been stuck at v336; corrected here. v336 - home AI refresh blocked when fewer than 3 prompts (no credit charged, toast 'Add at least 3 prompts to refresh'); pricing logic unchanged. v335 - internal page-scoped refresh (ivaDoAirPage) now falls back to the page's last snapshot prompts when DataForSEO returns no ranked keywords, so refresh always has 3 prompts. v334 - internal AI: prompt table (block 2) now always shown even when empty (was hidden when no data), empty table says 'No queries checked for this page yet'. v333 - internal AI refresh rewritten from domain-scoped to PAGE-scoped (ivaDoAirPage): re-fetches this page top-3 keywords, runs 3 prompts across ChatGPT/Perplexity/Google AI, checks this page citation, writes a page-level snapshot (no longer clobbers homepage AI data); internal cost by prompts relevant to this page (base 1, matrix /10); internal button label from real cost. v332 - empty-state copy rewritten: Core and AI now say run Core Audit / AI Readiness first (no 'add keywords/prompts first' language), for home and internal. v331 - real inline CSV downloads for positions/backlinks/prompt-runs (was toast-only stub), home AI refresh button label now reflects the real computed cost, delete action wording unified to Remove. v330 - prompt table grouped (Added by you / Selected by IvaBot), new added on top; refresh cost hook (add-refresh charges only new unrun prompts, normal refresh charges whole list, 3 auto free only on first run); refresh modal shows brand mentions/citations/N prompts across all engines, dropped standalone Google AI Overview line. v328 - auto-prompts seeded as regular prompts (deletable, no regeneration), auto shown first with 'auto' badge, refresh cost counts only user-added prompts (3 auto free): 1+ceil(added/10). v327 - SEO score as third Core card (Positions/Backlinks/Score): reads snapshots.audit_score, status word Strong/Moderate/Weak, score-over-time trend, additive only. v326 - whitelist-gated IIFE, mockup in a full-screen iframe on /dashboard.
   v68: render runs exactly once (readiness poll for parent __supabase + re-entrancy lock); removed the visibilitychange full-rebuild that reset the open page/metric and re-animated the graph. Fixes the multi-render/jumping and random tab-switching at the root, not just masked.
   v69: first real domain + its first page now open by default (demo scaffolding no longer counted as prior-open state); pinned badge stays only on home pages. Page cited centered in the Show-all-prompt-runs modal. Prompts Refresh now warns like keywords when refreshed within 5 days. Keyword delete-x right-aligned to match the prompt table.
   v70: Show-all-prompt-runs modal header now centers with its cells (fixes misaligned Page cited dashes and engine dots). Backlinks summary copy is honest and non-contradictory (leads with linking pages shown, then totals). Delete-x column pinned to a fixed right-aligned width in both the keyword and prompt tables so the x's line up.
   v71: Core refresh now runs IN the dashboard (no bounce to /app, no second report): one charge via the unified wallet (charge_credit, 1 + ceil(added keywords/50)), fetches ranked keywords + page-level backlinks (summary + full live list up to 1000) + added-keyword positions, writes ONE core snapshot via insert_core_run->insert_snapshot (trigger sets page_url), then re-renders. Separate 'Refresh keywords' button folded in. Backlinks shown strictly page-level (count = links we list, 'N backlinks to this page', no domain totals). Buy Credits explainer line added. Confirmation modal reused with simple item list + 3-day repeat warning. AI section refresh is the next drop (v72).
   v72: Core refresh button now shows the REAL cost (1 + ceil(added keywords/50)) instead of a hardcoded '1'. Confirmation modal lists the number of keyword positions being refreshed. AI prompt block wrapped in the same .detail frame as the Core keyword block so both sections share one style. Removed the v70 last-child CSS rule that pushed the ADDED BY YOU / RANKED IN GOOGLE group labels to the right.
   v73: AI Readiness refresh now runs IN the dashboard too (ivaRefresh generalized to scope 'air'): brand mentions (llm_mentions) + prompt citations (llm_responses x3 engines) + Google AI Overview (ai_overview) in one button, one charge via the unified wallet, one ai_readiness snapshot via insert_core_run->insert_snapshot. The home AI 'Refresh prompts' button is now the AI section refresh. Cost formula changed so keywords/prompts are INCLUDED in the base credit: Core = max(1, ceil(added keywords/50)) and AI = max(1, ceil(prompts/10)) - the first keyword/prompt no longer adds a whole credit. Buy Credits copy generalized to all tracked data.
   v74: AI refresh now costs 2 credits per 10 prompts (minimum 2) so it never runs at a loss (each prompt = 3 AI-engine calls). Core unchanged at max(1, ceil(added/50)).
   v75: AI cost smoothed to max(2, 1 + ceil(prompts/10)) - starts at 2 for up to 10 prompts, then +1 per additional 10 (no 2->4 jump). Core unchanged.
   v76: AI Readiness refresh button moved up to the section header (next to 'AI Readiness \u00b7 AI assistants'), mirroring Core's 'Refresh' at 'Core \u00b7 Google search'; removed from the 'Prompt tracking' prompt sub-block. Refresh confirmation now counts ALL tracked keywords in the list (added + ranked, including zero-position ones) via a data-kwtotal attribute, instead of only keywords that currently rank.
   v77: AI cost scales faster with prompts - max(2, ceil(prompts/5)) (1 credit per 5 prompts, min 2) so price keeps pace with the 3-engine-calls-per-prompt cost and margin does not erode on heavy prompt users. Previously +1 per 10, which grew slower than cost.
   v78: AI back to max(2, 1 + ceil(prompts/10)) - cheaper for users, still ~80% margin at real DataForSEO prices, to grow adoption of the flagship AI Readiness tool. Core keyword volume/difficulty (the $0.09 search_volume call) is now fetched only ONCE per keyword (when vol is still null) and cached, instead of on every refresh - drops repeat-refresh Core cost to ~$0.11 (~89% margin) without any unbilled add-time cost. Positions still refresh every time.
   v79 (GO-LIVE): whitelist removed - allowed() now returns true, dashboard open to all authenticated users. Keyword count card shows ALL tracked keywords (order.length), matching the list and the refresh modal, instead of only keywords that currently rank. Refresh pills restyled black (#151415) like the Buy Credits button so they stand out.
   v80: empty-state 'Run Core Audit' / 'Run AI Readiness' buttons were demo toasts; now they open the Track-a-new-page modal with the chosen tool pre-selected, which launches the tool directly via autorun (new window, writes snapshot, PDF-ready). openTrack() accepts an optional pre-selected tool. The tracked-page Run buttons and Track-a-new-page already launched the tool directly; unchanged.
   v81 (credits display fix): the real credits loader C() ran once on DOMContentLoaded before the parent window.__supabase/__userId were ready, bailed out, and left the hardcoded demo value 133 showing. C() now retries every 250ms (up to ~6s, like render) until the parent client is ready, then loads the real credits_balance. Initial creditNum placeholder changed from 133 to an em-dash; removed the demo setView clobber that forced creditNum to 3/133.
   v82 (profile wiring): the whole profile was demo-hardcoded ('Malina Arikh', avatar 'MA', and the founder's own email gal4eeenok@gmail.com shown to every user - a leak). Added PR() that loads the real logged-in user (sb.auth.getUser -> email + user_metadata name) with the same readiness retry as credits, and populates the avatar initials, account name, and Settings first/last name + email fields. Wired the demo-stub buttons to real Supabase auth: Save Name (updateUser data), Save Email (updateUser email), Save Password (updateUser password), Forgot Password (resetPasswordForEmail), and Log out (signOut + redirect home).
   v83 (reset-password flow): sendReset now passes redirectTo=https://ivabot.xyz/dashboard?reset=1 so the reset link lands on the dashboard instead of the homepage. New RC() handler detects arrival via recovery link (?reset=1 query, type=recovery hash, or the PASSWORD_RECOVERY auth event) and auto-opens Profile Settings focused on the New Password field, where Save Password (updateUser) completes the reset. NOTE: the dashboard URL must be added to Supabase Auth -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs.
   v84: password UX clearer for reset. Added a Confirm New Password field (pfNewPw2); Save Password now requires the two to match. In reset mode _openReset hides the Current Password row (not needed when you forgot it); normal openProfile shows it again.
   v22: pages with Core data now show the mockup's VERBATIM data-state panel (clickable kw/bl/ai/cite/aio cards + detail panels), filled from snapshots: keyword table = positions per keyword across snapshot dates (from ranked_keywords history), backlink table = real list (from backlinks jsonb), card counts real; AI-mention/citation detail panels show a "prompt tracking coming" note (no invented data). Pages without Core data keep the empty block. Delete crosses (×) restored on domains + pages (dashboard-view only, no DB delete). No charts (the mockup uses trend tables, not SVG). First domain + first page open.
   v21: verbatim per-page widget block (empty state).
   v9: dashboard nav is a plain STATIC top bar (does not move/stick, does not collapse) so it never covers metrics.
   Keeps all original items (Dashboard / Buy Credits / History / Settings / Log out); no Run IvaBot, no floating pill.
   The floating pill stays on the homepage only. Carried: scope-aware refresh modal + prompts note, footer logo eyes,
   email hello@ivabot.xyz, reveal cascade. iframe srcdoc = exact mockup doc, <base target="_parent">. Demo data. */
